<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.9.5: Using Verbal Descriptions to Create Graphs of Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01011</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.9.5: Using Verbal Descriptions to Create Graphs of Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>367cbb0f-9079-4c64-8da8-c6af15f622f4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para>To prepare for a backyard party, a parent uses two identical hoses to fill a small pool that is 15 inches deep and a large pool that is 27 inches deep.</para>
      <para>The height of the water in each pool is a function of time since the water is turned on.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Two pools.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_12_10_01.png"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </para>
      <para>Here are descriptions of three situations. For each situation, sketch the graphs of the two functions on the same coordinate plane, so that <emphasis>S(t)</emphasis> is the height of the water in the small pool after <emphasis>t</emphasis> minutes, and <emphasis>L(t)</emphasis> is the height of the water in the large pool after <emphasis>t</emphasis> minutes.</para>
      <para>In both functions, the height of the water is measured in inches.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
        <item>Situation 1: Each hose fills one pool at a constant rate. When the small pool is full, the water for that hose is shut off. The other hose keeps filling the larger pool until it is full.</item>
      </list>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Sketch your interpretation of the graph of situation 1.</para>
            <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Two functions. time in minutes and height in inches.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_12_10_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
        <item>Situation 2: Each hose fills one pool at a constant rate. When the small pool is full, both hoses are shut off.</item>
      </list>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Sketch your interpretation of the graph of situation 2.</para>
            <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Two functions. time in minutes and height in inches.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_12_10_03.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
        <item>Situation 3: Each hose fills one pool at a constant rate. When the small pool is full, both hoses are used to fill the large pool until it is full.</item>
      </list>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Sketch your interpretation of the graph of situation 3.</para>
            <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Two functions. time in minutes and height in inches.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_12_10_04.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
    </section>
  </content>
